{{also|Anfängen}}
==German==

===Etymology===
From {{inh|de|gmh|anvahen||to grab on}}, from {{m|gmh|an||on}} + {{m|gmh|vahen||to grab}}, corresponding to {{prefix|an|fangen|lang=de}}.

===Pronunciation===
* {{IPA|/ˈanˌfaŋən/|[ˈanˌfaŋən]|[ˈanˌfaŋŋ̩]|lang=de}}
* {{audio|De-anfangen.ogg|Audio|lang=de}}
* {{hyphenation|an|fan|gen|lang=de}}
* {{audio|De-at-anfangen.ogg|audio (Austria)|lang=de}}

===Verb===
{{de-verb-strong|class=7|fängt an|fing an|angefangen|auxiliary=[[haben]] or [[sein]]}}

# {{lb|de|intransitive}} to [[begin]]; to [[commence]]
#: ''Das Konzert '''fängt''' gleich '''an'''.''
#:: The concert is '''beginning''' shortly.
# {{lb|de|intransitive|with {{l|de|zu}} + infinitive}} to [[start]] doing something
#: ''Wann hast du '''angefangen''' zu rauchen?''
#:: When did you '''start''' smoking?
# {{lb|de|intransitive|with {{l|de|mit}}}} to [[begin]] something; to [[start]] something
#: ''Morgen '''fangen''' wir mit dem Projekt '''an'''.''
#:: Tomorrow we are '''starting''' the project.
# {{lb|de|transitive|colloquial}} to [[begin]] something; to [[start]] something
#: ''Wann hast du das Rauchen '''angefangen'''?''
#:: When did you '''start''' smoking?
#: ''Morgen '''fangen''' wir das Projekt '''an'''.''
#:: Tomorrow we are '''starting''' the project.

====Usage notes====
* The normal auxiliary for this verb is {{m|de|haben}}: ''Ich habe angefangen.'' – "I have begun." The auxiliary {{m|de|sein}} is used by some speakers in western and northern Germany: ''Ich bin angefangen.'' This is, however, widely restricted to colloquial usage. (Compare the same construction in Dutch {{m|nl|beginnen||to begin}}: ''Ik ben begonnen.'')
* The construction of ''anfangen'' as a transitive verb (sense 4) is perfectly acceptable in spoken German, but would not be common in formal writing.

